     8	
     9	## Strategy
    10	
    11	Two deliberate narrowings:
    12	
    13	1. **A `dao.stream` namespace, not an in-place rewrite.** The legacy
    14	   `dao.stream` has 52 `open!` call sites and every subsystem as a
    15	   consumer; rewriting it in place is an atomic big bang. The new
    16	   namespace is `dao.stream` — one new file,
    17	   `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc` — building the contract greenfield while
    18	   legacy keeps the system running.
    19	   Consumers migrate one at a time, later, under their own plans. The
    20	   legacy implementation and its tests are evidence about behavior, not
    21	   constraints: no compatibility facade, alias, or dual protocol.
    22	2. **The WebSocket slice, end to end, and nothing else.** One vertical
    23	   slice that forces the contract's riskiest promises early: the
    24	   descriptor round trip across a real serialization boundary, Model A
    25	   serving, writer-only surfaces, deposit admission, and observable
    26	   teardown. Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, RPC) wait until
    27	   this slice stands.
    28	
    29	The slice still requires a minimal reader-surface core — the served stream
    30	and the deposit destination are both ring buffers — so the ring buffer is
    31	in scope, but only what the contract requires of it, nothing the legacy
    32	one happens to have.
    33	
    34	## Phase 1 — Contract core (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`)
    35	
    36	The protocol and data surface, with no transport:
    37	
    38	- The seven operations as protocols/functions: `create!`, `attach!`,
    39	  `descriptor`, `cursor`, `next`, `append!`, `close!`. Surfaces are
    40	  protocol subsets (reader, writer, closable); a handle implements what
    41	  its transport declares.
    42	- The result convention: every operation returns an outcome map keyed by
    43	  `:dao.stream/outcome`, open maps, all qualified keywords under
    44	  `:dao.stream/…`, per-operation closed outcome sets exactly as the
    45	  contract's tables state (including the reserved `:dao.stream/denied`
    46	  and `:dao.stream/invalid-value`).
    47	- Generic envelope validation: `:dao.stream/type` present and qualified;
    48	  everything else transport-owned.
    49	- No ambient anything: no multimethod, no registry, no load-time side
    50	  effects. Host dispatch is documentation plus an example map in tests —
    51	  DaoStream ships no dispatch machinery.
    52	
    53	Deliverable: a contract-conformance test suite written against the
    54	protocols, parameterized by transport, so every later transport runs the
    55	same suite. Tests assert outcome shapes, cursor provenance (mint /
    56	successor / gap-recovery only), non-destructive multi-reader reads, and
    57	close scope.
    58	
    59	## Phase 2 — Ring buffer reference (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`)
    60	
    61	The reference reader+writer+closable transport, scoped to the contract:
    62	
    63	- Evict-oldest retention with declared capacity; `append!` never returns
    64	  `full`; monotonic retention; `gap` with earliest-retained-else-tail
    65	  recovery cursor.
    66	- Cursor anchors `:dao.stream/oldest` and `:dao.stream/newest`;
    67	  `invalid-anchor`; cursors carry logical-stream identity
