   113	  ring buffer is the slice's composition partner (deposit destination,
   114	  served stream), wired by the host composition in tests, not by the
   115	  transport. A transport requiring another transport is the legacy
   116	  registry defect reborn.
   117	
   118	Host coverage: clj and cljs at minimum (the cljd ws can follow the
   119	pattern once the slice is proven; beware the `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)`
   120	reader-conditional trap).
   121	
   122	## Phase 5 — The slice, end to end
   123	
   124	One test that is the point of the whole plan: two processes. Process A
   125	creates a ring buffer, serves it over ws, appends. Process B receives the
   126	descriptor (as data, over a stream), attaches, appends outbound; B's
   127	host boundary deposits inbound traffic into B's medium; an interpreter in
   128	B reads it with cursors. Kill the connection; verify detachment is
   129	observable on both sides, the served stream survives, and re-`attach!`
   130	with the same descriptor plus a kept cursor resumes.
   131	
   132	## Boundary of this plan
   133	
   134	Not in this plan, by design:
   135	
   136	- Migration of any consumer (dao.runtime, yin.vm, dao.jing, dao.space)
   137	  — each gets its own plan against the finished slice.
   138	- Other transports (file, UDP, log, relation, apply/RPC layers).
   139	- The waiter/readiness extension, Shibi, resumption protocol, cursor
   140	  serialization across hosts — deferred in the contract, deferred here.
   141	
   142	## End condition — v2 is not a permanent fork
   143	
   144	The v2 namespace exists to protect the running system during migration,
   145	not to live forever. When the slice is complete and consumers have
   146	migrated (under their own plans), legacy `dao.stream` is deleted and a
   147	single decision is taken explicitly: `dao.stream` is renamed to
   148	`dao.stream`, or keeps its name permanently. An undecided coexistence of
   149	both namespaces is a defect of the migration, not a steady state.
     1	# DaoStream Design
     2	
     3	Status: agreed design target. This document is the DaoStream contract. It does
     4	not redesign Yin.VM. Yin.VM is a downstream consumer and will be addressed only
     5	after this contract is implemented and tested.
     6	
     7	## Purpose
     8	
     9	DaoStream is the passive substrate for IO in datom.world. A stream carries
    10	values. It does not decide what those values mean, which interpreter observes
    11	them, or when an interpreter runs.
    12	
    13	The design separates four concepts:
    14	
    15	1. A **creation specification** is data for creating a new logical stream.
    16	2. A **handle** is a host-local operational stream value — the
    17	   implementation of the `dao.stream` protocols.
    18	3. A **portable descriptor** is data used to attach to an existing stream.
    19	4. A **cursor** is immutable interpreter-owned observation state for one
    20	   logical stream.
    21	
    22	Creation is not attachment. A descriptor is not a creation specification. A
    23	handle is not portable identity. A cursor is not handle-owned state.
    24	
    25	## Invariants
    26	
    27	- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.
    28	- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.
    29	- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.
    30	- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.
    31	- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.
    32	- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase
    33	  retained history.
    34	- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap
    35	  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.
    36	- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.
    37	
    38	Every clause below derives from these invariants. A proposed addition that
    39	cannot be derived from one of them does not belong in this contract.
    40	
    41	## Result Convention
    42	
    43	Every DaoStream operation returns data.
    44	
    45	- Each operation has a closed set of outcomes. An outcome is a map whose
    46	  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every
    47	  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and
    48	  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.
    49	- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per
    50	  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is
    51	  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be
    52	  added without breaking existing consumers.
    53	- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of
    54	  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an
    55	  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,
    56	  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions
    57	  and never callbacks.
    58	- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`. (The legacy
    59	  `:daostream/gap` spelling is retired.)
    60	
    61	## Creation and Attachment
    62	
    63	Creation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an
    64	existing one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.
    65	
    66	- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a
    67	  new logical stream. The handle's portable identity is available as a
    68	  descriptor via `descriptor`.
    69	- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on the
    70	  **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching never creates.
    71	- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only
    72	  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a
    73	  request to load a plugin.
    74	
    75	Streams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization
    76	boundary. An in-memory stream holds references, so a handle can ride it
    77	intact, and an interpreter that receives a handle simply uses it —
    78	every operation takes a handle, and attaching to what you already hold
    79	is meaningless. But a handle does not survive encoding, and any
    80	transport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —
    81	is a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those
    82	boundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a
    83	transport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a
    84	value it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)
    85	`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and
    86	resolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the
    87	streams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the
    88	files a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no
    89	registry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving
    90	somewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other
    91	host, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —
    92	honestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.
    93	
    94	### Envelopes
    95	
    96	A **creation specification** is a map with one required key:
    97	
    98	- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the
    99	  dispatch key.
   100	
   101	Every other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace
   102	(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler
   103	— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its
   104	own keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.
   105	
   106	The **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object
   107	reference. Its exact definition is not designed yet; these properties are
   108	settled, and the key set that realizes them is TBD:
   109	
   110	- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch
   111	  key.
   112	- It names exactly one logical stream, and that identity is stable: the same
   113	  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.
   114	- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is
   115	  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is
   116	  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns
   117	  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.
   118	- Like an IOR, it may carry authorization: an optional `:dao.stream/shibi`
   119	  key holding Shibi capability tokens that gate whether an interpreter may
   120	  attach. Shibi is not yet designed or built; until it is, the key is
   121	  reserved, no attachment is gated, and the `:dao.stream/denied` outcome
   122	  reserved in the `attach!` table below is unreachable.
   123	
   124	For the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`
   125	on a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,
   126	a value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end,
   127	where `attach!` on the reconstructed value reaches the stream it names —
   128	provided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and
   129	Attachment on reachability).
   130	
   131	Both envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive
   132	the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is
   133	structural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or
   134	identity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —
   135	a consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.
   136	
   137	`create!` outcomes:
   138	
   139	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |
   140	|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   141	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists.                  | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   142	| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |
   143	| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |
   144	| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |
   145	
   146	`attach!` outcomes:
   147	
   148	| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |
   149	|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   150	| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | Attached to the existing logical stream.                                            | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   151	| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |
   152	| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |
   153	| `:dao.stream/denied`             | Attachment refused by capability gating (reserved; unreachable until Shibi exists). | —                    |
   154	| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |
   155	
   156	### Host Dispatch
   157	
   158	When a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —
   159	for example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on
   160	`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's
   161	`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:
   162	
   163	```clojure
   164	{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!
   165	                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}
   166	 :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/create ws/create!
   167	                         :dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}
   168	```
   169	
   170	This table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an
   171	ordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor
   172	standardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns
   173	`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.
   174	
   175	Code that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions
   176	directly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global
   177	registry and no namespace-load registration side effect. (The legacy `open!`
   178	multimethod and `defopen` macro are retired.)
   179	
   180	## Surfaces
   181	
   182	A handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can
   183	honor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**
   184	(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and
   185	Retention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.
   186	
   187	The reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as
   188	one positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and
   189	re-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that
   190	promise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the
   191	host's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary
   192	— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a
   193	transport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the
   194	transport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per
   195	its declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly
   196	defect. (`dao.stream.ws.md` specifies one such transport and the admission
   197	rule in detail.)
   198	
   199	## Cursors
   200	
   201	A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.
   202	
   203	- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**
   204	  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract
   205	  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained
   206	  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended
   207	  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport
   208	  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional
   209	  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is
   210	  malformed or not honored by this transport yields
   211	  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,
   212	  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream
   213	  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains
   214	  readable.
   215	- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as
   216	  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never
   217	  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A
   218	  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.
   219	- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the
   220	  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter
   221	  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives
   222	  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the
   223	  descriptor envelope.
   224	- A cursor carries enough identity to detect misuse — the mismatch outcomes
   225	  in the Reading table depend on it.
   226	- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never
   227	  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a
   228	  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.
   229	- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and
   230	  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a
   231	  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.
   232	
   233	## Reading
   234	
   235	`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.
   236	It is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data
   237	answer.
   238	
   239	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |
   240	|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   241	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |
   242	| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |
   243	| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |
   244	| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |
   245	| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |
   246	| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |
   247	
   248	Reading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same
   249	position observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the
   250	other. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no
   251	take. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one
   252	reader's progress every other reader's data loss.
   253	
   254	## Writing
   255	
   256	`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:
   257	
   258	| Outcome                     | Meaning                                                                                                                     | Required keys |
   259	|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
   260	| `:dao.stream/ok`            | The value is accepted at the next position in the stream's sequence. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |
   261	| `:dao.stream/full`          | The transport cannot accept the value now; nothing was appended.                                                            | —             |
   262	| `:dao.stream/invalid-value` | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                            | —             |
   263	| `:dao.stream/closed`        | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                | —             |
   264	
   265	The writer decides what to do about full or closed; DaoStream does not retry,
   266	buffer, or notify on its behalf.
   267	
   268	## Close
   269	
   270	`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome is
   271	`:dao.stream/ok`. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope is also
   272	`:dao.stream/ok`.
   273	
   274	`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle that owns a stream — its
   275	creator, or its server-side host — closes the logical stream. An attached
   276	handle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the
   277	stream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin
   278	later. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared
   279	nature.
   280	
   281	Outcome meanings are handle-relative throughout: `:dao.stream/closed` and
   282	`:dao.stream/end` speak of what *this handle* is on. After an attachment
   283	closes, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers
   284	`ok` — identity outlives any attachment; a reader surface, where the
   285	handle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this
   286	handle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are
   287	minted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,
   288	not to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.
   289	
   290	The bullets below describe closing the logical stream.
   291	
   292	Close changes future availability only:
   293	
   294	- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.
   295	- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead
   296	  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.
   297	- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a
   298	  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.
   299	
   300	There is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are
   301	authoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.
   302	
   303	## Concurrency
   304	
   305	Each logical stream is one append-only sequence: a successfully appended
   306	value occupies exactly one definite position in that sequence, and every
   307	operation's outcome reflects the sequence at one moment. DaoStream
   308	guarantees nothing further about concurrent operations.
   309	
   310	This follows from the second axiom of datom.world — data is syntax;
   311	semantics emerge through interpretation. The sequence is the one truth; each
   312	outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A reader that observes
   313	`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a
   314	contradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global
   315	view exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is
   316	answered by the sequence itself: the value has a position or it does not.
   317	What an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —
   318	a merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,
   319	and interpretation happens above the stream.
   320	
   321	## Retention and Gaps
   322	
   323	Retention and overflow behavior are transport-owned, declared by the
   324	creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts
   325	the oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send
   326	buffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —
   327	`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or
   328	permanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the
   329	outcomes and standardizes no policy.
   330	
   331	When bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the
   332	gap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides
   333	nothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live
   334	telemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently
   335	skipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader must be able to know it
   336	missed values.
   337	
   338	## Composition
   339	
   340	DaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything
   341	that transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its
   342	own cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely
   343	outside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the
   344	pipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.
   345	
   346	## Readiness and Polling
   347	
   348	This contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives
   349	`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the
   350	concern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.
   351	
   352	A future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on
   353	transports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for
   354	example a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already
   355	permits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of
   356	this contract may depend on it existing.
   357	
   358	## Public Surface
   359	
   360	| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |
   361	|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|
   362	| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |
   363	| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |
   364	| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |
   365	| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |
   366	| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |
   367	| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |
   368	| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |
   369	
   370	A handle implements the subset of these operations its transport
   371	declares (see Surfaces).
   372	
   373	Explicitly absent, by derivation from the invariants: `open!` and `defopen`
   374	(ambient registry), `closed?` (stale predicate), `drain-one!` and any
   375	destructive read (readers advance cursors, not the stream), `seek`
   376	(every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and kept
   377	cursors cover repositioning), `take!!` (blocking), waiter registration
   378	(no readiness extension), and throwing conveniences such as `strict-vec`
   379	(operational outcomes are data).
     1	# DaoStream WebSocket Transport
     2	
     3	Status: design target, subordinate to `dao.stream.md`. That document is the
     4	contract; this one specifies the WebSocket transport's implementation of it.
     5	Where the two disagree, the contract wins.
     6	
     7	## What the Descriptor Names
     8	
     9	A WebSocket descriptor names a **server-hosted stream**, never a connection.
    10	The stream exists on the serving host — created there, retained there,
    11	closed there by its owner — and it exists whether or not any client is
    12	currently connected. A connection is the mechanics of one attachment:
    13	connecting joins the stream, disconnecting detaches from it, and neither
    14	event creates, closes, or destroys anything. That is what makes `attach!`
    15	honest here: the same descriptor reaches the same stream every time, because
    16	the stream's identity and lifecycle belong to the server, not to the wire.
    17	
    18	This framing generalizes: **serving is what makes any stream remotely
    19	attachable.** A ring buffer is host-local only because nothing speaks for
    20	it. A host that composes a WebSocket endpoint in front of one is serving
    21	that ring buffer; its descriptor now carries an address, and remote
    22	interpreters can attach. The transport owns no stream — it serves streams
    23	the host composed, which is exactly the contract's rule that descriptors
    24	resolve against "the streams a network endpoint serves because serving them
    25	is what it is."
    26	
    27	A server that wants per-conversation state does not bend attachment into
    28	creation: it `create!`s a fresh stream for the conversation and offers that
    29	stream's descriptor.
    30	
    31	## Surfaces
    32	
    33	A WebSocket handle is **writer** and **closable** only. It has no
    34	reader surface: a socket retains nothing, so it cannot make the reader
    35	surface's promise — a positioned, append-only, retained sequence that
    36	cursors can observe and re-observe. `cursor` and `next` do not exist on it.
    37	
    38	## The Duplex Model
    39	
    40	A WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the
    41	design keeps them separate:
    42	
    43	- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and
    44	  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.
    45	- **Inbound**: each arriving message is a host event. The host's adapter
    46	  deposits it — payloads and connection lifecycle events alike, judging
    47	  nothing — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this
    48	  boundary. Per ADR 0003 there is no separate event bus: dao.space is the
    49	  medium. From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,
    50	  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that
    51	  stream.
    52	
    53	The deposit destination is wired once, when the host composes the boundary
    54	— it is not a parameter of `attach!`, and no attaching caller chooses or
    55	knows it. Deposited events carry the attachment's identity, so every
    56	interpreter takes its own perspective on the same deposited truth: one
    57	indexes the traffic, another copies it to a file, a third ignores it. How
    58	arrivals are interpreted is the interpreter's business, not the
    59	transport's configuration.
    60	
    61	```clojure
    62	(def sock (-> (ws/attach! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ws
    63	                           :ws/host "example.org"
    64	                           :ws/port 9090
    65	                           :ws/path "/streams/telemetry"})
    66	              :dao.stream/handle))
    67	
    68	;; write: (append! sock msg)
    69	;; read:  interpreters observe the medium the host boundary deposits into,
    70	;;        filtering on this attachment's identity
    71	```
    72	
    73	Nothing accumulates inside the transport: a hidden per-socket inbox would
    74	be a retention window with an eviction policy nobody composed, sized by
    75	nobody. Retention lives in the medium the boundary deposits into, composed
    76	and sized by the host.
    77	
    78	There are no callback hooks. The legacy `on-open!` / `on-message!` /
    79	`on-close!` surface is retired: every socket event becomes data on the
    80	deposit stream — payload and lifecycle (opened, closed by peer, error)
    81	alike, distinguishable by shape. A boundary deposits everything; whether
    82	anyone reads it is not the boundary's concern.
    83	
    84	### Deposit Admission
    85	
    86	The adapter runs in a callback context: it cannot wait, cannot park, and
    87	cannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the
    88	deposit destination must be one that always admits, and this is enforced
    89	where it can be — at assembly, against the destination's declared nature:
    90	
    91	- The destination must declare **evict-oldest retention with a
    92	  host-declared capacity**. It never answers `:dao.stream/full`; under
    93	  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s at lagging readers — the one
    94	  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an
    95	  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the
    96	  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed
    97	  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting
    98	  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,
    99	  like any storage failure.)
   100	- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused
   101	  when the boundary is composed — the same category as supplying both
   102	  dispatch shapes. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before
   103	  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.
   104	- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit
   105	  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is
   106	  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination
   107	  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;
   108	  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary
   109	  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.
   110	  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the
   111	  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and
   112	  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary
   113	  `attach!`.
   114	
   115	Sizing the window is choosing how much reader lag to tolerate before gaps
   116	appear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Where
   117	the destination transport supports it, the window may be resized live
   118	(a transport-owned operation on the owning handle): growing it only slows
   119	the eviction horizon, shrinking it is ordinary early eviction reported as
   120	gaps, and positions never renumber. An adaptive policy — grow on rising
   121	gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own telemetry,
   122	not a transport behavior.
   123	
   124	The accounting is then complete: overload is a reported `gap`, a dead
   125	destination is an observable disconnection, and silent loss is
   126	unconstructible.
   127	
   128	## Envelope
   129	
   130	Transport-owned keys, qualified under `:ws/…`. The exact key set is TBD
   131	alongside the portable descriptor's definition in the contract; settled
   132	properties:
   133	
   134	- The descriptor is self-contained and exists to cross the network: it
   135	  carries what any host needs to reach the served stream — host and port,
   136	  plus the served stream's identity (a path or stream id; endpoint address
   137	  alone does not name a stream), perhaps TLS parameters.
   138	- The descriptor carries identity only. Where inbound traffic lands is the
   139	  host boundary's composition (see The Duplex Model) and appears in no
   140	  envelope and no argument of `attach!`.
   141	
   142	## Operations
   143	
   144	- `attach!` connects. `:dao.stream/not-found` is reserved for an
   145	  authoritative answer that what the descriptor names does not exist — the
   146	  endpoint responds and disclaims the stream. Transient reachability and
   147	  connection failures — DNS, timeout, routing, refusal — are
   148	  `:dao.stream/transport-error`, so a caller's retry policy is
   149	  deterministic: `not-found` is not a transient failure to retry
   150	  automatically (though a server may serve that identity later — retrying
   151	  on external evidence is interpreter policy), while retrying
   152	  `transport-error` may succeed on its own.
   153	- `append!` returns `:dao.stream/full` when the send buffer cannot accept
   154	  the value now — **transient**: the network drains the buffer on its own,
   155	  and a later append succeeds. `:dao.stream/closed` when the connection is
   156	  down. A value that cannot be encoded for the wire is
   157	  `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.
   158	- `close!` on an attached handle means **disconnect from the server**: the
   159	  handle is on the attachment, so closing it ends the connection,
   160	  idempotently, and this handle's subsequent `append!` returns
   161	  `:dao.stream/closed`. The served stream is untouched — closing *it*
   162	  belongs to its server-side owner. Disconnection is not final: the
   163	  descriptor remains valid and `attach!` rejoins the same stream. The
   164	  peer's boundary deposits the departure into its host-composed event
   165	  medium, correlated by attachment identity.
   166	- Connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited: the served stream
   167	  persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` with the same
   168	  descriptor rejoins the same stream.
   169	
   170	## Elements and Serialization
   171	
   172	The wire is a serialization boundary. Every element must survive the codec
   173	structurally unchanged; a handle can never travel — its portable
   174	descriptor can.
   175	
   176	## Deferred
   177	
   178	- Exact envelope key set (tracks the contract's descriptor TBD).
   179	- Resumption protocol: stream identity is stable across reconnects, so
   180	  resumption is possible by design; what history a rejoining client receives
   181	  (and how it states where it left off) is not yet specified.
   182	- Backpressure protocols above the stream (a reader publishing its progress
   183	  as data is interpretation, outside both this transport and the contract).
   184	- Relationship to the RPC layers built over streams.

