    61	## Creation and Attachment
    62	
    63	Creation brings a new logical stream into existence. Attachment joins an
    64	existing one. They are separate operations with separate inputs.
    65	
    66	- `create!` consumes a creation specification and returns a handle on a
    67	  new logical stream. The handle's portable identity is available as a
    68	  descriptor via `descriptor`.
    69	- `attach!` consumes a portable descriptor and returns a handle on the
    70	  **same** logical stream the descriptor names. Attaching never creates.
    71	- A creation specification says how to build a stream. A descriptor only
    72	  names one — never executable code, never a construction program, never a
    73	  request to load a plugin.
    74	
    75	Streams are values that can be sent through streams — up to a serialization
    76	boundary. An in-memory stream holds references, so a handle can ride it
    77	intact, and an interpreter that receives a handle simply uses it —
    78	every operation takes a handle, and attaching to what you already hold
    79	is meaningless. But a handle does not survive encoding, and any
    80	transport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —
    81	is a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those
    82	boundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a
    83	transport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a
    84	value it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)
    85	`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and
    86	resolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the
    87	streams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the
    88	files a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no
    89	registry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving
    90	somewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other
    91	host, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —
    92	honestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.
    93	
    94	### Envelopes
    95	
    96	A **creation specification** is a map with one required key:
    97	
    98	- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the
    99	  dispatch key.
   100	
   101	Every other key is transport-owned, qualified under the transport's namespace
   102	(for example a ring buffer's capacity), and validated by the selected handler
   103	— generic validation checks only the envelope, and a handler that rejects its
   104	own keys returns `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`.
   105	
   106	The **portable descriptor** is inspired by CORBA's interoperable object
   107	reference. Its exact definition is not designed yet; these properties are
   108	settled, and the key set that realizes them is TBD:
   109	
   110	- It carries `:dao.stream/type` — the transport, as above — as its dispatch
   111	  key.
   112	- It names exactly one logical stream, and that identity is stable: the same
   113	  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.
   114	- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is
   115	  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is
   116	  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns
   117	  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.
   118	- Like an IOR, it may carry authorization: an optional `:dao.stream/shibi`
   119	  key holding Shibi capability tokens that gate whether an interpreter may
   120	  attach. Shibi is not yet designed or built; until it is, the key is
   121	  reserved, no attachment is gated, and the `:dao.stream/denied` outcome
   122	  reserved in the `attach!` table below is unreachable.
   123	
   124	For the time being, the operative guarantee is the round trip: `descriptor`
   125	on a live handle returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/descriptor`,
   126	a value that can be sent on a stream and reconstructed on the other end,
   127	where `attach!` on the reconstructed value reaches the stream it names —
   128	provided the receiving host can reach what it names at all (see Creation and
   129	Attachment on reachability).
   130	
   131	Both envelopes are plain data through and through: every value must survive
   132	the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, so equality is
   133	structural, and no key holds a function, a host object, a live handle, or
   134	identity smuggled in metadata. Like every DaoStream map, envelopes are open —
   135	a consumer ignores qualified keys it does not understand.
   136	
   137	`create!` outcomes:
   138	
   139	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                       | Required keys        |
   140	|-------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   141	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A new logical stream exists.                  | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   142	| `:dao.stream/invalid-spec`    | The creation specification is malformed.      | —                    |
   143	| `:dao.stream/not-found`       | No transport here matches `:dao.stream/type`. | —                    |
   144	| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The handler failed.                           | —                    |
   145	
   146	`attach!` outcomes:
   147	
   148	| Outcome                          | Meaning                                                                             | Required keys        |
   149	|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------|
   150	| `:dao.stream/ok`                 | Attached to the existing logical stream.                                            | `:dao.stream/handle` |
   151	| `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` | The descriptor is malformed.                                                        | —                    |
   152	| `:dao.stream/not-found`          | The descriptor names no stream here, or no transport matches its type.              | —                    |
   153	| `:dao.stream/denied`             | Attachment refused by capability gating (reserved; unreachable until Shibi exists). | —                    |
   154	| `:dao.stream/transport-error`    | The handler failed.                                                                 | —                    |
   155	
   156	### Host Dispatch
   157	
   158	When a creation specification or portable descriptor arrives dynamically —
   159	for example off a stream — the host looks at it and dispatches on
   160	`:dao.stream/type`. The type resolves to a map that has the transport's
   161	`:dao.stream/create` and `:dao.stream/attach` implementations:
   162	
   163	```clojure
   164	{:dao.stream/ringbuffer {:dao.stream/create ringbuffer/create!
   165	                         :dao.stream/attach ringbuffer/attach!}
   166	 :dao.stream/ws         {:dao.stream/create ws/create!
   167	                         :dao.stream/attach ws/attach!}}
   168	```
   169	
   170	This table is ordinary data owned by the host, and the dispatch is an
   171	ordinary map lookup done by the host — DaoStream neither performs nor
   172	standardizes it. A host that cannot dispatch on `:dao.stream/type` returns
   173	`:dao.stream/not-found` — never creation or discovery.
   174	
   175	Code that knows its transport statically calls the transport's functions
   176	directly; no dispatch is involved. There is no ambient namespace-global
   177	registry and no namespace-load registration side effect. (The legacy `open!`
   178	multimethod and `defopen` macro are retired.)
   179	
   180	## Surfaces
   181	
   182	A handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can
   183	honor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**
   184	(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and
   185	Retention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.
   186	
   187	The reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as
   188	one positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and
   189	re-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that
   190	promise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the
   191	host's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary
   192	— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a
   193	transport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the
   194	transport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per
   195	its declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly
   196	defect. (`dao.stream.ws.md` specifies one such transport and the admission
   197	rule in detail.)
   198	
   199	## Cursors
   200	
   201	A cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.
   202	
   203	- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**
   204	  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract
   205	  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained
   206	  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended
   207	  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport
   208	  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional
   209	  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is
   210	  malformed or not honored by this transport yields
   211	  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,
   212	  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream
   213	  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains
   214	  readable.
   215	- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as
   216	  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never
   217	  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A
   218	  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.
   219	- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the
   220	  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter
   221	  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives
   222	  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the
   223	  descriptor envelope.
   224	- A cursor carries enough identity to detect misuse — the mismatch outcomes
   225	  in the Reading table depend on it.
   226	- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never
   227	  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a
   228	  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.
   229	- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and
   230	  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a
   231	  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.
   232	
   233	## Reading
   234	
   235	`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.
   236	It is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data
   237	answer.
   238	
   239	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |
   240	|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   338	## Composition
   339	
   340	DaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything
   341	that transforms one stream into another is an interpreter — it reads via its
   342	own cursor and appends to an ordinary output stream — and that lives entirely
   343	outside this contract, needing no support from it. As in a Unix pipeline, the
   344	pipe is dumb; the transforming happens in the processes on either end.
   345	
   346	## Readiness and Polling
   347	
   348	This contract has no readiness or waiter extension. A reader that receives
   349	`:dao.stream/blocked` retries `next` when it chooses; retry cadence is the
   350	concern of the interpreter or the runtime driving it, not of the stream.
   351	
   352	A future readiness extension would be additive: an optional protocol on
   353	transports that support it, plus optional keys in operation results (for
   354	example a wake token list on `append!`), which the open-map rule already
   355	permits. Nothing in this contract needs to change for that; no consumer of
   356	this contract may depend on it existing.
   357	
   358	## Public Surface
   359	
   360	| Operation    | Consumes               | Yields             |
   361	|--------------|------------------------|--------------------|
   362	| `create!`    | creation specification | handle outcome     |
   363	| `attach!`    | portable descriptor    | handle outcome     |
   364	| `descriptor` | handle                 | descriptor outcome |
   365	| `cursor`     | handle, anchor         | cursor outcome     |
   366	| `next`       | handle, cursor         | read outcome       |
   367	| `append!`    | handle, value          | write outcome      |
   368	| `close!`     | handle                 | close outcome      |
   369	
   370	A handle implements the subset of these operations its transport
   371	declares (see Surfaces).
   372	
   373	Explicitly absent, by derivation from the invariants: `open!` and `defopen`
   374	(ambient registry), `closed?` (stale predicate), `drain-one!` and any
   375	destructive read (readers advance cursors, not the stream), `seek`
   376	(every valid cursor comes from the stream; anchored minting and kept
   377	cursors cover repositioning), `take!!` (blocking), waiter registration
   378	(no readiness extension), and throwing conveniences such as `strict-vec`
   379	(operational outcomes are data).
