   233	## Reading
   234	
   235	`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.
   236	It is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data
   237	answer.
   238	
   239	| Outcome                       | Meaning                                                                       | Required keys                                                                           |
   240	|-------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
   241	| `:dao.stream/ok`              | A value was observed.                                                         | `:dao.stream/value`, `:dao.stream/cursor` (successor)                                   |
   242	| `:dao.stream/blocked`         | Nothing at this position yet; retry later.                                    | —                                                                                       |
   243	| `:dao.stream/end`             | What this handle is on is closed and this position is past the retained tail. | —                                                                                       |
   244	| `:dao.stream/gap`             | This position was evicted.                                                    | `:dao.stream/cursor` (earliest retained position, or the tail when nothing is retained) |
   245	| `:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch` | The cursor was minted by a different logical stream.                          | —                                                                                       |
   246	| `:dao.stream/invalid-cursor`  | The value is not a cursor.                                                    | —                                                                                       |
   247	
   248	Reading never mutates the stream. Two readers with cursors at the same
   249	position observe the same retained value; neither can starve or affect the
   250	other. There is no destructive read anywhere in the contract — no drain, no
   251	take. An operation that removes an element as it reads would make one
   252	reader's progress every other reader's data loss.
   253	
   254	## Writing
   255	
   256	`append!` takes a handle and a value and returns an outcome:
   257	
   258	| Outcome                     | Meaning                                                                                                                     | Required keys |
   259	|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
   260	| `:dao.stream/ok`            | The value is accepted at the next position in the stream's sequence. Implies neither local readability nor remote delivery. | —             |
   261	| `:dao.stream/full`          | The transport cannot accept the value now; nothing was appended.                                                            | —             |
   262	| `:dao.stream/invalid-value` | The transport cannot carry this value (for example, it cannot be encoded); nothing was appended.                            | —             |
   263	| `:dao.stream/closed`        | What this handle is on is closed to new appends; the value was not appended.                                                | —             |
   264	
   265	The writer decides what to do about full or closed; DaoStream does not retry,
   266	buffer, or notify on its behalf.
   267	
   268	## Close
   269	
   270	`close!` takes a handle and returns a map whose outcome is
   271	`:dao.stream/ok`. It is idempotent: closing an already-closed scope is also
   272	`:dao.stream/ok`.
   273	
   274	`close!` closes **what the handle is on**. A handle that owns a stream — its
   275	creator, or its server-side host — closes the logical stream. An attached
   276	handle is on an attachment: closing it ends that attachment only — the
   277	stream persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` may rejoin
   278	later. Which scope a transport's handles carry is part of its declared
   279	nature.
   280	
   281	Outcome meanings are handle-relative throughout: `:dao.stream/closed` and
   282	`:dao.stream/end` speak of what *this handle* is on. After an attachment
   283	closes, the handle's `append!` answers `closed`; `descriptor` still answers
   284	`ok` — identity outlives any attachment; a reader surface, where the
   285	handle has one, answers `end` from `next` once observation through this
   286	handle is exhausted, and `closed` from `cursor` — no new cursors are
   287	minted through a closed attachment. Cursors bind to the logical stream,
   288	not to a handle, so a kept cursor resumes through a newly attached handle.
   289	
   290	The bullets below describe closing the logical stream.
   291	
   292	Close changes future availability only:
   293	
   294	- Subsequent `append!` returns `:dao.stream/closed`.
   295	- A reader that exhausts retained history receives `:dao.stream/end` instead
   296	  of `:dao.stream/blocked`.
   297	- Retained history remains readable. Close erases nothing; eviction is a
   298	  retention concern, not a lifecycle one.
   299	
   300	There is no `closed?` predicate in the public surface. Operation results are
   301	authoritative; a predicate answer is stale the moment it is returned.
   302	
   303	## Concurrency
   304	
   305	Each logical stream is one append-only sequence: a successfully appended
   306	value occupies exactly one definite position in that sequence, and every
   307	operation's outcome reflects the sequence at one moment. DaoStream
   308	guarantees nothing further about concurrent operations.
   309	
   310	This follows from the second axiom of datom.world — data is syntax;
   311	semantics emerge through interpretation. The sequence is the one truth; each
   312	outcome is one perspective on it at one moment. A reader that observes
   313	`:dao.stream/blocked` and then a value has not caught the stream in a
   314	contradiction — it made two observations from its own cursor, and no global
   315	view exists to contradict. Whether an append landed before a close is
   316	answered by the sequence itself: the value has a position or it does not.
   317	What an ordering between writers *means*, and any coordination among them —
   318	a merge interpreter, a one-log-per-writer discipline — is interpretation,
   319	and interpretation happens above the stream.
   320	
   321	## Retention and Gaps
   322	
   323	Retention and overflow behavior are transport-owned, declared by the
   324	creation specification. A ring buffer retains a bounded window and evicts
   325	the oldest; a file or append-only log retains everything; a network send
   326	buffer refuses transiently. Which outcomes a given stream can produce —
   327	`full` on append, `gap` on read — and whether its `full` is transient or
   328	permanent follow from the transport's declared nature. DaoStream names the
   329	outcomes and standardizes no policy.
   330	
   331	When bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the
   332	gap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides
   333	nothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live
   334	telemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently
   335	skipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader must be able to know it
   336	missed values.
